# Operation vocabulary for diagnostic translation. See the POD below.
# English descriptions follow regen/opcodes. Translated entries are drafts.

use utf8;

=pod

=head1 OPERATION WORDS

This config is separate from diag_conf.pl because the shared operation
vocabulary grows independently of individual diagnostic sentences.
The file returns a hash reference; it does not set globals in its caller.

The version 2 schema separates a language profile, reusable word rules,
and diagnostic bindings. Regeneration compiles complete phrases and rule
outputs into language-specific numeric lookup tables. Rendering selects a
temporary word view without changing canonical diagnostic parameters.

=head2 Language profiles

Each language declares C<base_form>, an ordered C<forms> list, C<tags>,
C<rules>, and C<words>. Form names are local to that language. For example:

    base_form => 'default',
    forms     => [qw(default definite)],
    tags      => { gender => [qw(masculine feminine)] },

C<default> is a lookup convention, not a grammatical gender. Another
language may call its base form C<nominative>. Each word supplies text in
C<forms>, optional grammatical C<tags>, and optional C<status> (C<draft> or
C<reviewed>). Every supplied form and tag must be declared by the profile.
A word need only provide the forms its actual diagnostic rules request.
German C<indefinite> phrases are nominative; its C<accusative> and
C<dative> forms include the indefinite article. Icelandic case forms have
no article. The other current non-English profiles use C<indefinite>
phrases alongside their bare C<default> form. Forms contain the complete
phrase, including agreement and elision. No morphology runs at render time.
Missing words remain explicit incomplete coverage.

English word entries may include C<description>, source-audited guidance for
translators when the short opcode label is ambiguous. This guidance is part
of translation provenance and is never substituted into rendered messages.

English base forms preserve descriptions from F<regen/opcodes>. Literal
keyword names and the explicit C<literal_operations> aliases pass through
unchanged, including complete syntax examples such as C<eval {block}>.
They have no translated word entry. C<opaque_operations> lists identities
whose text belongs to an extension, such as C<custom>. They retain their
supplied text rather than a fixed core translation.

All ten non-English dictionaries cover the descriptive domain in draft
form. Coverage is not linguistic review. See
F<planning/diagnostic-operation-vocabulary-audit.md> for provenance, forms,
phrase roles and outstanding review questions.

=head2 Word rules

A rule requests a word form and independently selects grammatical outputs:

    subject => {
        form   => 'nominative',
        select => { gender => { tag => 'gender', fallback => '*' } },
    },

The rule reads a declared tag. A missing tag uses the explicit fallback,
which must be C<*> or a declared value of that tag. C<select =E<gt> {}>
requests text without a grammatical selector. There are no executable
expressions, conditional branches or runtime grammar interpreter in this
initial contract. Add conditional form choice when a real sentence needs it.

=head2 Diagnostic bindings

In F<diag_conf.pl>, each C<operation_words> entry binds a displayed parameter:

    target => {
        source     => 'target_operation',
        operations => $META->{lvalue_word_target},
        rules      => { en => 'operation', fr => 'indefinite' },
        selectors  => {},
    },

C<source> names a retained internal message input, separately from the
word's displayed parameter and the grammatical outputs. C<operations> is
its audited operation domain, not the entire opcode list. Shared domain
arrays live in C<$META> so dumping and reloading preserves references.
Optional C<assert_unknown =E<gt> 1> preserves a debug assertion for a
source-bounded domain. Unknown identities retain their supplied text in
release builds. Missing translations request English fallback and do not
violate the domain assertion.
C<rules> chooses a language-local rule for each participating language.
English is required; a missing language binding cannot silently borrow a
rule from another language.

C<selectors> maps rule output names to distinct diagnostic dimensions.
For example, C<gender =E<gt> 'target_gender'> sends a rule's gender result to
the target's dimension. Another word has its own C<context_gender>.
The possible values are unioned across language rules for that dimension.
Form names do not become selector values. Regeneration adds the derived
dimensions to the existing selector layout. They are not extra diagnostic
parameters and are reconstructed from the rules when the config reloads.

=head2 Resolution and translation context

C<Diag::Words::resolve_word> is a pure Perl reference resolver. It returns
C<status>, and when available, C<text>, C<form> and C<selectors>. Status is
one of C<resolved>, C<literal>, C<opaque>, C<unknown_language>,
C<unknown_operation>, C<missing_word> or C<missing_form>.
Missing text leaves language fallback to the caller. Opaque custom text and
literal names preserve their supplied spelling without inferred gender.
Resolution never changes canonical words, bindings or diagnostic parameters.
Binding validation rejects missing forms on translated words where a rule
actually needs them, identifying the diagnostic, language, operation and rule.

Translation packets share each relevant word once across parameter domains.
They contain the requested language's profile and used rules plus English
fallback rules. Forms, tags and bindings contribute to source fingerprints;
another language's unrelated changes do not invalidate the request.
The packet reports C<runtime_substitution =E<gt> 1>. Numeric identity
inputs use one plus the opcode, reserving zero for unknown. The synthetic
C<do_block> identity follows the opcode table. Identity is retained outside
grammatical selector fields; it is not a template parameter.

=cut

